    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The transcription factor p53, which can activate or repress gene expression, is known to be critical for suppressing tumorigenesis in humans and mice. Known as the guardian of the genome, p53 responds to DNA damage by inducing apoptosis, cell cycle arrest and senescence. p53 is a well characterized transcription factor involved in diverse cellular pathways, but its full protein interactome remains undefined. The Attardi lab has performed dual affinity purification and mass spectrometry to identify novel binding partners of mouse p53 and has developed a protein interactome for p53. One of the proteins identified in this interactome is Mettl3, which is a methyltransferase enzyme involved in the modification of RNA. It has been implicated in cellular differentiation, cell self-renewal and DNA-damage responses. We validated the interaction of p53 and Mettl3 by performing co-immunoprecipitation assays. Our data indicate that p53 does indeed co-immunoprecipitate with Mettl3 and corroborate the interaction between them as shown by affinity purification and mass spectrometry studies. We have shown that Mettl3 plays an important role in modulating p53 stability, and that Mettl3 deficiency results in decreased expression of several key p53 target genes in response to DNA damage. The findings in this study validate that Mettl3 interacts with p53 and that this interaction may have important implications in p53 biology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
